fn main() {
    let s = String::from("hello");

    let len = calculate_length(&s); // borrow, ownership stays with s
    println!("{s} has {len} bytes"); // s still valid

    let moved = s;                  // move: s is no longer usable
    // println!("{s}");            // compile error: value borrowed after move
    println!("{moved}");
}

fn calculate_length(s: &str) -> usize {
    s.len()
}
```

Every value has exactly one owner. Passing by value moves ownership and the old binding becomes invalid, which is how Rust prevents double frees without a GC. Taking &str instead of &String accepts both a String and a literal, and borrows rather than moves. You may have any number of immutable borrows or exactly one mutable borrow, never both, which rules out data races at compile time.

