```solidity
contract Priced {
    uint256 public constant PRICE_SCALE = 1e18;

    error AmountTooSmall();
    error SlippageExceeded();

    /// cost = amount * price / PRICE_SCALE, rounded down in the protocol's favour.
    function cost(uint256 amount, uint256 pricePerUnit) public pure returns (uint256) {
        return (amount * pricePerUnit) / PRICE_SCALE;
    }

    function buy(uint256 amount, uint256 pricePerUnit, uint256 minOut) external payable {
        uint256 owed = cost(amount, pricePerUnit);
        uint256 out = (amount * PRICE_SCALE) / pricePerUnit;
        if (out < minOut) revert SlippageExceeded();
        if (owed == 0) revert AmountTooSmall();
        // ... transfer
    }
}
```

Three rules. Multiply before dividing so you lose precision once, not twice. Round consistently in one direction, here down for cost and up for what the user receives, and never let rounding favour the user or a dust attack appears. Add explicit minimums and zero checks, because integer division can return 0 for a nonzero amount and silently break accounting. For tokens with arbitrary decimals, rescale to a common precision rather than assuming 18.

